Part 1: How to Run a Service Exclusively on a Internal Network?

To restrict a service so that it only runs on an internal network and is completely isolated from the public internet, you can use the following
methods:
   
Binding to Local/Private IP Addresses: Configure the service (like Node.js, Python, or Go apps, or Nginx) to listen strictly on 
a private IP address (such as 127.0.0.1 or 192.168.x.x) instead of binding to 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces). 
This ensures external network interfaces cannot reach it directly.

Firewall Rules (iptables / UFW): Set up operating system or cloud firewall rules to block any incoming traffic to the service's 
port from external IP addresses, only allowing traffic originating from the local subnet or trusted internal gateway.

Docker Network Isolation: Run the legacy service inside a Docker container attached only to a private Docker bridge network (
without exposing or publishing its ports to the host machine using the -p flag), meaning only containers on the same internal network bridge 
can talk to it.

Part 2: What is TLS Termination?

TLS Termination (or SSL termination) is the process of decrypting incoming HTTPS traffic at the edge (usually at a reverse proxy, 
load balancer, or proxy server) before passing it along to the backend/legacy service.

    The Philosophy & Purpose: Legacy or internal services are often built to handle plain, unencrypted HTTP traffic internally to save 
CPU resources and simplify configuration. However, exposing plain HTTP over the public internet is a major security risk. TLS termination allows 
the proxy to handle all the heavy cryptographic lifting (managing certificates, handling HTTPS handshakes, encryption/decryption) and send clean, 
unencrypted HTTP requests to the legacy service over a trusted, isolated internal network.
